@chapter Calling Eightbol from Assembly

@cindex calling from assembly
@cindex native assembly
@cindex interop

This chapter describes how to call Eightbol-compiled methods and routines
from hand-written assembly code. Understanding these conventions is essential
when you need to integrate performance-critical code, access hardware
directly, or bridge Eightbol's object-oriented layer with lower-level
routines.

@menu
* Why Call from Assembly::         Use cases and motivations
* Method Call Protocol::           How Eightbol dispatches method calls
* Parameter Passing::              How data moves between assembly and Eightbol
* Return Values::                  Getting results back
* Calling by CPU::                  Conventions for each processor
* Service Bank Calls::             The service dispatch system
* Examples::                        Working assembly examples
@end menu

@node Why Call from Assembly
@section Why Call from Assembly

@cindex use cases
@cindex inline assembly
@cindex performance

You may need to call Eightbol methods from assembly in several scenarios:

@itemize
@item Integrating hardware access code that cannot be expressed in
higher-level languages
@item Hot paths where hand-optimized assembly outperforms generated code
@item Calling existing assembly libraries or system routines that Eightbol
does not directly support
@item Debugging by instrumenting method entry/exit from assembly wrappers
@end itemize

Eightbol's OOPS system uses a consistent calling convention across all
backends: a single return value passes through the machine's primary
register, and all other data moves through global variables.

@node Method Call Protocol
@section Method Call Protocol

@cindex OOPS
@cindex method dispatch
@cindex DoCallMethod

Eightbol uses a message-passing OOPS (Object-Oriented Programming System)
model inspired by Smalltalk and Phantasia. Every method is a labeled
subroutine following these rules:

@itemize
@item Method entry label follows the OOPS convention:
@code{Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}}
@item Hyphenated COBOL names become underscores:
@code{Song--Heal--ID} becomes @code{MethodSongHealID}
@item Methods receive the object pointer in a global variable named
@code{Self}
@item Methods return via the platform's standard return instruction
(@code{rts}, @code{ret}, @code{jr}, etc.)
@end itemize

@subsubsection The DoCallMethod Dispatcher

On the 6502 family, method calls route through a dispatcher macro:

@example
.CallMethod Call@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}, @var{ClassName}Class
@end example

This macro loads the method's dispatch address from the class's method
table and jumps to it. The class pointer is passed to allow superclass
method lookup.

@node Parameter Passing
@section Parameter Passing

@cindex parameters
@cindex globals

Eightbol passes parameters through global variables, not the stack. This
simplifies calling conventions and matches the 8-bit tradition of
register-starved architectures.

@itemize
@item Single arguments: passed in the calling-convention register (A on
6502, A on Z80, R0 on ARM7, D0 on m68k)
@item Multiple arguments: first in the calling register, rest in global
variables
@item Arrays and structures: a pointer to the data in the calling register
or a global
@end itemize

@subsubsection Global Variables

All class slots and method-local temporaries are accessed through the
@code{Self} pointer for instance data, or as absolute addresses for
globals. The slot offset is known at compile time from the copybook
definition.

@node Return Values
@section Return Values

@cindex return values
@cindex accumulator

Every backend returns a single value in the machine's primary register:

@table @asis
@item 6502 / 65c02 / 65c816 / HuC6280 / RP2A03
@code{A} (accumulator, 8-bit) or @code{A/X} combined (16-bit little-endian)
@item Z80 / SM83
@code{A} (8-bit) or @code{HL} (16-bit)
@item cp1610
@code{R0} (low byte for 8-bit)
@item m68k
@code{D0} (data register 0)
@item ARM7
@code{R0}
@item F8
@code{A} (accumulator)
@item i286
@code{AX}
@end table

If a method returns no value, the register contents after the call are
undefined.

@node Calling by CPU
@section Calling Conventions by CPU

Each CPU family has specific conventions that hand-written assembly must
follow to interoperate with Eightbol code.

@menu
* 6502 Calling::                   6502, 65c02, 65c816, HuC6280, RP2A03
* Z80 Calling::                   Zilog Z80
* SM83 Calling::                  Sharp SM83 (Game Boy)
* cp1610 Calling::                Intellivision
* m68k Calling::                  Motorola 68000
* ARM7 Calling::                  ARM7TDMI (Game Boy Advance)
* F8 Calling::                    Fairchild F8 (Channel F)
* i286 Calling::                   Intel 80286
@end menu

@node 6502 Calling
@subsection 6502 Family Calling Convention

@cindex 6502 calling convention

@subsubsection Register Usage

@table @asis
@item @code{A}
Calling-convention register. Holds 8-bit arguments and return values.
@item @code{X}
16-bit operations combine A (low) with X (high).
@item @code{Y}
Slot offset index for object slot access.
@item @code{S}
Stack pointer (page 1, 256 bytes).
@end table

@subsubsection Method Invocation

To call an Eightbol method from 6502 assembly:

@example
;; Set Self pointer to your object
        lda #<MyObject
        sta Self
        lda #>MyObject
        sta Self+1

;; Call the method
        jsr     MethodGameHeroTakeDamage
@end example

@subsubsection Passing Arguments

For unary calls (argument in A):

@example
        lda     #5                      ; argument = 5
        jsr     MethodGameHeroHeal
        sta     Result                  ; return value in A
@end example

@subsubsection Method Entry

Eightbol methods begin with slot access using Y-indexed addressing:

@example
MethodGameHeroTakeDamage:
        ldy     #SlotOffsetOfHealth     ; health field
        lda     (Self),y                ; load current health
        sbc     DamageAmount            ; subtract damage
        sta     (Self),y                ; store back
        rts
@end example

@node Z80 Calling
@subsection Zilog Z80 Calling Convention

@cindex Z80 calling convention

@subsubsection Register Usage

@table @asis
@item @code{A}
Calling-convention register. Holds 8-bit arguments and return values.
@item @code{HL}
16-bit register pair. Holds 16-bit arguments, return values, and
addresses.
@item @code{BC}
Count register for loops and string operations.
@item @code{DE}
Destination pointer and temporary.
@end table

@subsubsection Method Invocation

@example
;; Set Self pointer
        ld      hl, MyObject
        ld      (Self), hl

;; Call method
        call    MethodGameHeroTakeDamage
        ld      a, l                   ; return value in L for 16-bit
@end example

@subsubsection Passing Arguments

For 8-bit arguments in A:

@example
        ld      a, 5                   ; argument
        call    MethodGameHeroHeal
        ld      (Result), a            ; return value in A
@end example

For 16-bit arguments in HL:

@example
        ld      hl, 1234h              ; 16-bit argument
        call    MethodSomeRoutine
        ld      (Result), hl           ; return value in HL
@end example

@node SM83 Calling
@subsection Sharp SM83 (Game Boy) Calling Convention

@cindex SM83 calling convention

The SM83 uses the same conventions as the Z80, with RGBDS syntax:

@example
;; Set Self pointer
        ld      hl, MyObject
        ld      [Self], hl

;; Call method
        call    MethodGameHeroTakeDamage
        ld      a, l                   ; return value
@end example

@node cp1610 Calling
@subsection Intellivision cp1610 Calling Convention

@cindex cp1610 calling convention

@subsubsection Register Usage

@table @asis
@item @code{R0}–@code{R7}
16-bit general-purpose registers.
@item @code{R5}
Return address register. @code{JSR R5, addr} pushes return; @code{JR R5} returns.
@item @code{R6}
Stack pointer.
@end table

@subsubsection Method Invocation

@example
;; Set Self pointer (R4 is conventional for object pointer)
        MVII    #MyObject, R4
        MVO     R4, Self

;; Call method
        JSR     R5, MethodGameHeroTakeDamage

;; Return value in R0 (low byte for 8-bit)
        MVO     R0, Result
@end example

@subsubsection Passing Arguments

@example
;; 16-bit argument in R0
        MVII    #5, R0
        JSR     R5, MethodGameHeroHeal
        MOVR    R0, R1                 ; copy return value
@end example

@node m68k Calling
@subsection Motorola 68000 Calling Convention

@cindex m68k calling convention

@subsubsection Register Usage

@table @asis
@item @code{D0}
Calling-convention register. Holds arguments and return values.
@item @code{D1}–@code{D7}
Data registers for temporaries.
@item @code{A0}
Address temporary for pointer arithmetic.
@item @code{A7}
Stack pointer.
@end table

@subsubsection Method Invocation

@example
;; Set Self pointer
        lea     Self, %a0
        move.l  %a4, (%a0)            ; assuming object in A4

;; Call method
        jsr     MethodGameHeroTakeDamage

;; Return value in D0
        move.l  %d0, Result
@end example

@subsubsection Passing Arguments

@example
;; 16-bit argument in D0
        move.w  #5, %d0
        jsr     MethodGameHeroHeal
        move.w  %d0, Result
@end example

@node ARM7 Calling
@subsection ARM7TDMI Calling Convention

@cindex ARM7 calling convention
@cindex Thumb

@subsubsection Register Usage

@table @asis
@item @code{r0}
Calling-convention register. Holds arguments and return values.
@item @code{r1}–@code{r3}
Scratch registers for temporaries.
@item @code{r13} (SP)
Stack pointer.
@item @code{r14} (LR)
Link register. Holds return address from @code{bl} calls.
@end table

@subsubsection Method Invocation

@example
        .thumb_func
        .global CallGameHeroTakeDamage
CallGameHeroTakeDamage:
        ldr     r1, =Self
        str     r4, [r1]              ; assuming object in r4

        bl      MethodGameHeroTakeDamage
        bx      lr                     ; return to caller
@end example

@subsubsection Passing Arguments

@example
        movs    r0, #5                ; argument in r0
        bl      MethodGameHeroHeal
        ldr     r1, =Result
        strb    r0, [r1]              ; store return value
@end example

@node F8 Calling
@subsection Fairchild F8 Calling Convention

@cindex F8 calling convention

@subsubsection Register Usage

@table @asis
@item @code{A}
8-bit accumulator. Calling-convention register.
@item @code{DC0}
16-bit data counter. Used for memory addressing.
@item @code{IS}
Index stack pointer for scratch register access.
@end table

@subsubsection Method Invocation

@example
;; Set Self pointer (using scratch registers 10/11 for 16-bit)
        LI      #<MyObject
        LR      10, A
        LI      #>MyObject
        LR      11, A

;; Store to Self global
        DCI     Self
        LM                      ; load high byte of Self
        LR      11, A
        LM                      ; load low byte
        LR      10, A

;; Call method
        PI      MethodGameHeroTakeDamage
@end example

@subsubsection Passing Arguments

@example
        LI      5                     ; argument in A
        PI      MethodGameHeroHeal
        LR      10, A                 ; store return value
@end example

@node i286 Calling
@subsection Intel 80286 Calling Convention

@cindex i286 calling convention

@subsubsection Register Usage

@table @asis
@item @code{AX}
Calling-convention register. Holds arguments and return values.
@item @code{BX}, @code{SI}, @code{DI}
Temporaries and addressing.
@item @code{CX}
Count register for loops.
@end table

@subsubsection Method Invocation

@example
;; Set Self pointer
        mov     ax, seg Self
        mov     ds, ax
        mov     bx, [bp+4]            ; object address from stack

        mov     ax, seg Self
        mov     ds, ax
        lea     si, Self
        mov     [si], bx

;; Call method
        call    MethodGameHeroTakeDamage
        mov     [Result], ax
@end example

@node Service Bank Calls
@section Service Bank Calls

@cindex service bank
@cindex dispatch
@cindex .Dispatch

Service banks provide a layer-1 dispatch mechanism for calling routines
across bank boundaries. The system uses a Look-Up Table (LUT) that maps
service names to bank numbers.

@menu
* Service Bank LUT::              How the dispatch table works
* Calling Services from Assembly:: Invoking services from hand-written code
@end menu

@node Service Bank LUT
@subsection Service Bank LUT

The service bank LUT is built from @code{.Dispatch} directives in bank
assembly files. Each dispatch entry maps a service name to a bank:

@example
.Dispatch        ServiceAudio, AudioBank
.Dispatch        ServiceVideo, VideoBank
.Dispatch        ServiceInput, InputBank
@end example

When Eightbol encounters @code{CALL SERVICE Audio}, it looks up
@code{ServiceAudio} in the LUT, finds bank @code{AudioBank}, and emits
a far call:

@example
.FarJSR  ServiceAudio, AudioBank
@end example

@node Calling Services from Assembly
@subsection Calling Services from Assembly

To call a service from hand-written assembly, use the same dispatch
mechanism:

@example
;; 6502 example: call Audio service
        .FarJSR  ServiceAudio, AudioBank
@end example

The service routine receives any arguments through the standard calling
convention and returns results the same way.

@node Examples
@section Examples

This section provides complete, working examples of calling Eightbol
methods from native assembly for each supported CPU.

@menu
* 6502 Examples::                 Working 6502 assembly examples
* Z80 Examples::                  Working Z80 assembly examples
* ARM7 Examples::                 Working ARM7 Thumb examples
@end menu

@node 6502 Examples
@subsection 6502 Assembly Examples

@subsubsection Calling a Method with No Arguments

@example
;; 6502 (64tass syntax)
;; Assume: GameHero object at label HeroObject

        ;; Set up Self pointer
        lda     #<HeroObject
        sta     Self
        lda     #>HeroObject
        sta     Self+1

        ;; Call the method
        jsr     MethodGameHeroDie

        ;; Continue with execution
        rts
@end example

@subsubsection Calling a Method with Return Value

@example
;; 6502: Call Heal method, get result in accumulator

        ;; Set up Self pointer
        lda     #<HeroObject
        sta     Self
        lda     #>HeroObject
        sta     Self+1

        ;; Call the method
        jsr     MethodGameHeroHeal

        ;; Return value is in A
        sta     HealthAfterHeal
        rts
@end example

@subsubsection Calling a Method with Argument

@example
;; 6502: Call TakeDamage with argument in A

        lda     #5                      ; damage amount = 5
        jsr     MethodGameHeroTakeDamage

        ;; Return value in A (remaining health)
        sta     NewHealth
        rts
@end example

@node Z80 Examples
@subsection Z80 Assembly Examples

@subsubsection Calling a Method with No Arguments

@example
; Z80 (sjasmplus syntax)
; Assume: GameHero object at label HeroObject

        ld      hl, HeroObject
        ld      (Self), hl

        call    MethodGameHeroDie

        ret
@end example

@subsubsection Calling a Method with Return Value

@example
; Z80: Call Heal method, get result in L (16-bit) or A (8-bit)

        ld      hl, HeroObject
        ld      (Self), hl

        call    MethodGameHeroHeal

        ld      a, l                   ; for 16-bit result
        ld      (HealthAfterHeal), a
        ret
@end example

@subsubsection Calling a Method with Argument

@example
; Z80: Call TakeDamage with 8-bit argument in A

        ld      a, 5                   ; damage amount
        call    MethodGameHeroTakeDamage

        ld      (NewHealth), a
        ret
@end example

@node ARM7 Examples
@subsection ARM7 Thumb Examples

@subsubsection Calling a Method with No Arguments

@example
        .thumb
        .syntax unified
        .arch armv4t
        .cpu arm7tdmi

        .thumb_func
        .global CallHeroDie
CallHeroDie:
        ldr     r1, =Self
        movs    r0, #<HeroObject
        lsls    r0, r0, #16
        adds    r0, #>HeroObject
        str     r0, [r1]

        bl      MethodGameHeroDie
        bx      lr
@end example

@subsubsection Calling a Method with Argument

@example
        .thumb_func
        .global CallHeroTakeDamage
CallHeroTakeDamage:
        @ Argument in r0 (damage amount)

        ldr     r1, =Self
        movs    r2, #<HeroObject
        lsls    r2, r2, #16
        adds    r2, #>HeroObject
        str     r2, [r1]

        bl      MethodGameHeroTakeDamage

        @ Return value in r0
        ldr     r1, =NewHealth
        strb    r0, [r1]
        bx      lr
@end example

